WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.920
So now finally, let's re-look at the projective camera projection matrix that we have seen

00:06.920 --> 00:14.320
earlier on, that there are altogether 11 degrees of freedom in the camera projection matrix,

00:14.320 --> 00:24.080
which is given by the K with 5 degrees of freedom from the intrinsics.

00:24.080 --> 00:33.920
And then another 3 plus 3 equals to 6 degrees of freedom from the extrinsics of the camera.

00:33.920 --> 00:39.000
So now the million dollar question is that, since we have altogether 11 unknowns that

00:39.000 --> 00:44.740
constitute this camera projection matrix, how can we find all the 11 parameters, which

00:44.740 --> 00:48.960
is the 11 degrees of freedom of the camera projection matrix?

00:48.960 --> 00:53.080
The answer is true calibration, and we will look at how to do this.

00:53.080 --> 00:58.960
Besides calling this procedure as the calibration of the camera, it's also very commonly known

00:58.960 --> 01:00.960
as camera re-sectioning.

01:00.960 --> 01:06.680
So re-sectioning simply means that we want to find the parameters that make up the camera

01:06.680 --> 01:12.640
projection matrix, the intrinsics as well as the extrinsics parameters.

01:12.640 --> 01:19.000
And one of the simplest approach and most commonly used approach is what we call the

01:19.000 --> 01:26.920
calibration, with a 2D calibration pattern that might look something like this with circles.

01:26.920 --> 01:32.160
But more commonly used would be the checkerboard pattern.

01:32.160 --> 01:38.840
You have to physically print this out and paste it onto a flat board, which is a plane,

01:38.840 --> 01:40.480
and we'll see why is this so.

01:40.480 --> 01:45.960
I'll be mainly describing the technique written by Kang Sen Yeo in this particular paper,

01:45.960 --> 01:52.580
a flexible new technique for camera calibration, that was published in Tipami in the year 2000.

01:52.580 --> 01:59.440
So here's some open source calibration toolboxes where you can download and play with it yourself.

01:59.440 --> 02:04.920
They are all mainly based on this paper that was published in Tipami in the year 2000.

02:04.920 --> 02:09.880
So the first one is the Bouguet calibration toolbox, commonly known as the Bouguet calibration

02:09.880 --> 02:14.000
toolbox, because it was written by Bouguet.

02:14.000 --> 02:16.560
So this was based on MATLAB.

02:16.560 --> 02:22.600
And then later, some people took this code and developed it into C++.

02:22.600 --> 02:27.660
And I think there's also a Python version right now in OpenCV.

02:27.660 --> 02:30.720
So you probably can find a later version.

02:30.720 --> 02:36.040
What I put here is version 2.4, but I think now it's probably version 3 point something

02:36.040 --> 02:38.240
already or maybe even version 4.

02:38.240 --> 02:43.560
This particular Bouguet calibration toolbox is also migrated into the MATLAB image processing

02:43.560 --> 02:44.560
toolbox.

02:44.560 --> 02:47.960
So these are free versions, this is also free.

02:47.960 --> 02:54.940
But this is a paid version because it's under MATLAB imaging processing toolbox.

02:54.940 --> 03:02.320
Let's describe the actual procedure for calibration of a projective camera with the checkerboard.

03:02.320 --> 03:06.840
So given a checkerboard that looks like this, we would have to first print out the checkerboard

03:06.840 --> 03:12.000
pattern and then we have to paste it on the flat planar board that looks something like

03:12.000 --> 03:13.000
this.

03:13.440 --> 03:18.200
The first thing that we need to do is to assign a reference frame onto the checkerboard.

03:18.200 --> 03:25.400
And we will conveniently select the assignment of this by saying that we will assign the

03:25.400 --> 03:36.320
xy plane to be on the plane of this checkerboard over here, such that the z-axis would be zero,

03:36.320 --> 03:42.920
the z-reading of all these points on the checkerboard would be always zero.

03:42.960 --> 03:47.440
The next thing is that we'll have to put this into our camera projection matrix, which is

03:47.440 --> 03:52.500
given by the equation x small x equals to p multiplied by x.

03:52.500 --> 03:59.000
And since we know that the projection is always up to a certain scale, since we have looked

03:59.000 --> 04:06.120
at earlier on as x as y and the unknown scale here can be actually factorized out such that

04:06.120 --> 04:11.240
this becomes a normalized homogeneous coordinate over here.

04:11.240 --> 04:21.120
And p over here would be rewritten into this form, r and t over here.

04:21.120 --> 04:27.960
So essentially this is equal to p and we will multiply the corners that we observe on the

04:27.960 --> 04:29.120
checkerboard.

04:29.120 --> 04:33.160
What we have seen earlier on is that the checkerboard looks something like this.

04:33.160 --> 04:34.920
So this part here is shaded.

04:34.920 --> 04:37.280
So we'll be able to detect these corners.

04:37.320 --> 04:45.680
So every corner will be represented by x, y, zero and one, since the world axis is conveniently

04:45.680 --> 04:52.840
attached to the checkerboard where the x-y plane is lying on the checkerboard itself.

04:52.840 --> 04:56.760
And this means that all the z-axis is equal to zero here.

04:56.760 --> 04:59.820
So that's why there's a zero here.

04:59.820 --> 05:06.040
So if we were to evaluate this, we'll see that this term here on the right hand side,

05:06.040 --> 05:12.280
this multiplication of the matrices and vectors over here, it ends up to be this equation

05:12.280 --> 05:21.240
over here where z can be ignored because the z is going to multiply by the third column

05:21.240 --> 05:22.800
of this guy over here.

05:22.800 --> 05:29.840
And hence we can ignore r3 in this equation over here as well as z equals to zero over

05:29.840 --> 05:30.840
here.

05:30.880 --> 05:37.800
Effectively, what we get here is that we have 2D projective mapping from the checkerboard,

05:37.800 --> 05:45.760
which is this is P2, and we actually get to map it into a P2 space, which is on the image.

05:45.760 --> 05:49.920
So this means we're doing a mapping from a plane to a plane, which simply means that

05:49.920 --> 05:56.200
the transformation operation here given by k, r1, r2, and t is simply a homography which

05:56.240 --> 06:04.920
can be represented by h1, h2, h3 over here, where h1, h2, h3 respectively are the vectors

06:04.920 --> 06:09.640
in each column of the homography matrix.

06:09.640 --> 06:12.680
And we can rewrite this into this form.

06:12.680 --> 06:20.680
The scale homography with the unknown scale factor here is equals to k multiplied by r1,

06:20.680 --> 06:22.960
r2, and t.

06:23.000 --> 06:25.200
This homography is unknown.

06:25.200 --> 06:32.880
x, y, 1 here, the image coordinate, this is known because given the image of the checkerboard,

06:32.880 --> 06:39.000
we can actually know where are the x, y coordinate of the image, as well as the capital X, Y

06:39.000 --> 06:44.920
coordinate over here, because we know that we attach the world frame onto the maybe let's

06:44.920 --> 06:50.520
say the top left hand corner of the checkerboard, and we know that the checkerboard is of a

06:50.520 --> 06:51.920
regular size.

06:51.920 --> 06:58.240
That means that I know that each square is probably one centimetre by one centimetre.

06:58.240 --> 07:03.680
So by looking at this, and I also know that how many squares are there in the checkerboard

07:03.680 --> 07:08.280
and where are the black and white boxes, so what it means is that I will be able to find

07:08.280 --> 07:14.760
the one-to-one correspondence of the 2D coordinate point, the corners, matching those corners

07:14.760 --> 07:18.400
at the checkerboard in the overall scene.

07:18.400 --> 07:20.040
So this part here is known.

07:20.040 --> 07:26.880
Now the only unknown would be the homography as well as the scale factor.

07:26.880 --> 07:38.420
So based on the known x, y in the image, as well as the known x, y in the world coordinate,

07:38.420 --> 07:44.960
so now the objective is to find the homography as well as the unknown scale factor.

07:44.960 --> 07:51.400
And we can do this by looking at the equation over here, that we know that this S multiplied

07:51.400 --> 08:01.200
by the homography is going to be equal to k R1, R2 and T. And we can simply equate this

08:01.200 --> 08:03.840
to get two independent constraints.

08:03.840 --> 08:11.240
So here I simply multiply S by H1 and then I bring k over, so this would be equal to

08:11.240 --> 08:17.560
R1 and this would be equal to R2, although we don't know what's R1 and R2 at this moment,

08:17.560 --> 08:25.320
but one thing that we know from here is that R1, R2, since it belongs to the rotation matrix,

08:25.320 --> 08:32.260
because this has to be R1, R2 and R3, and R1, R2 is the first two columns of the rotation

08:32.260 --> 08:37.200
matrix, and we can make use of what we have seen earlier on, the octonormal constraints

08:37.200 --> 08:42.960
of the entries of the columns in the rotation matrix to get a constraint.

08:42.960 --> 08:47.960
So this means that since they are both octagonal, this is perpendicular to each other, that

08:47.960 --> 08:52.680
means that the dot product of R1, R2 must be equal to zero, so we can apply this dot

08:52.680 --> 08:54.680
product and equate it to zero.

08:54.680 --> 09:00.560
What this means is that this term here, dot with this term here, they are going to be

09:00.560 --> 09:06.280
equal to zero, and hence we get the constraint over here, equation one, and we also know

09:06.360 --> 09:12.920
that since this is an octonormal constraint, the norm of these two vectors over here, R1

09:12.920 --> 09:19.240
and R2, they should be equal, hence we can write this relation over here, that the dot

09:19.240 --> 09:27.400
product of this R1 over here, which is equivalent to the norm of R1, is going to be equal to

09:27.400 --> 09:29.080
the norm of R2.

09:29.080 --> 09:34.620
We'll see that in the next lecture that this term here, this interesting term here, is

09:34.620 --> 09:40.860
equal to the image of the absolute conic, which we will call omega, in the next lecture.

09:40.860 --> 09:49.780
And now we got two equations over here, which consist of all the unknowns of the homography,

09:49.780 --> 09:54.700
as well as the intrinsic values.

09:54.700 --> 10:00.660
Let's denote this guy, K inverse transpose multiplied by K inverse as a 3-matrix, it's

10:00.660 --> 10:07.340
actually a 3x3 matrix, and since we are multiplying it by the transpose, or taking the inverse,

10:07.340 --> 10:12.620
this means that B here should be symmetrical as well as positive definite, it should be

10:12.620 --> 10:19.540
a positive definite matrix, because this can be seen as the square of a matrix.

10:19.540 --> 10:25.760
When we take a square, that means that the eigenvalues would have to be strictly positive.

10:25.760 --> 10:34.080
And since B is symmetrical, we can rewrite this in terms of a 6x1 vector of unknowns.

10:34.080 --> 10:38.600
Then we will get this represented by a small b over here.

10:38.600 --> 10:43.840
So finally, we can rearrange the equations in 1 and 2.

10:43.840 --> 10:52.240
If we compactly write this term K inverse transpose, K inverse, represented by the 6x1

10:52.240 --> 10:59.900
vector B, we can rewrite this into a linear constraint of the form of A B equals to 0.

10:59.900 --> 11:05.380
So similarly here, we can also rewrite, we can bring this guy over here, and then this

11:05.380 --> 11:08.840
will be equal to this term minus this term equals to 0.

11:08.840 --> 11:14.640
We can rewrite this K inverse transpose and K inverse into the 6x1 vector B, and then

11:14.640 --> 11:17.620
rewrite this into a linear constraint here.

11:17.620 --> 11:21.680
So if we were to stack these two equations together, we can rearrange this homography

11:21.680 --> 11:28.640
term into this form where A is made up of the homography terms, and B is made up of

11:28.640 --> 11:35.120
the unknowns from the intrinsic values over here, which is K.

11:35.120 --> 11:45.560
A is going to be a 2x6 matrix because B is a 6x1 vector, and we have two equations that

11:45.560 --> 11:46.840
we have seen earlier on.

11:46.840 --> 11:54.000
So all together, A would have 2x6 entries, and now this A is going to be made up of all

11:54.000 --> 11:58.920
the homography terms that we have seen earlier on in this equation over here.

11:58.920 --> 12:06.280
It consists of H1 and H2, and B is going to be made up of all the unknown terms in the

12:06.280 --> 12:12.920
camera matrix, which is K inverse transpose multiplied by K inverse.

12:12.920 --> 12:19.120
So what it means is that since we have 6 unknowns in this equation, AB equals to 0,

12:19.120 --> 12:24.880
where this has 6 unknowns, which one of the point correspondence here is going to give

12:24.880 --> 12:32.640
us a 2x6 entry in A. So all together, since we need to solve for 6 unknowns, all together

12:32.640 --> 12:35.000
we need a minimum of 3 different views.

12:35.000 --> 12:39.880
That means each view is going to give me H1 and H2, a homography that relates a view from

12:39.880 --> 12:41.360
the real checkerboard.

12:41.360 --> 12:48.400
So this is the checkerboard, it's mapped into the image.

12:48.400 --> 12:53.120
One view is going to give me H1 and H2, so let's denote this as 1.

12:53.120 --> 12:56.640
And then all together, I need 3 different views.

12:56.640 --> 13:01.840
I need to move either the checkerboard or the camera, I can fix the camera, and then

13:01.840 --> 13:05.280
take the checkerboard just like what this guy is doing.

13:05.280 --> 13:10.280
He's going to move this around into a second view, so this is the second view.

13:10.280 --> 13:14.520
And then we will get another set of correspondences that relates this homography.

13:14.520 --> 13:17.300
So we have to do this at least 3 different views.

13:17.300 --> 13:24.800
So this is the third view, where I will get another homography to the image.

13:24.800 --> 13:35.640
Now once we have this done, we all together have a 6x6 A matrix multiplied by a 6x1 unknown

13:35.640 --> 13:37.660
B and this is equal to 0.

13:37.660 --> 13:41.700
The first thing that we need to do is that we need to get A in order to solve for the

13:41.700 --> 13:47.100
unknown B and what we can do here is that we know that the mapping between the checkerboard

13:47.100 --> 13:52.780
and the image is given by a homography from lecture 3.

13:52.780 --> 13:57.900
We know that if we have 4 correspondences between each view, we will be able to solve

13:57.900 --> 14:02.060
for the homography using this equation over here.

14:02.060 --> 14:10.700
So we know that we have xy1 that's equal to a homography multiplied by xy1.

14:10.700 --> 14:14.500
We can ignore the scale because this is a homography up to scale.

14:14.500 --> 14:21.540
And so one view here is going to give us this correspondence and we need at least 4 point

14:21.540 --> 14:24.820
correspondences to solve for this homography over here.

14:24.820 --> 14:29.620
For each view, we have 4 correspondences, we will be able to solve for the homography

14:29.620 --> 14:36.100
and if we have at least 3 views over here, we will get 3 sets of constraints where we

14:36.100 --> 14:41.300
can solve for 3 different homographies and we can plug it into this equation to get a

14:41.300 --> 14:49.540
6x6 known A matrix multiplied by a 6x1 unknown vector that consists of an intrinsic value

14:49.540 --> 14:50.540
k.

14:50.540 --> 14:59.460
And then since we have A, B equals to 0, where A has to be 6x6 and then B has to be 6x1.

14:59.940 --> 15:08.340
So we can easily solve for the SVD of A and this is going to be U sigma V transpose.

15:08.340 --> 15:19.460
And then the solution of B is going to take the V vector that corresponds to the least

15:19.460 --> 15:23.500
singular value of this guy over here.

15:23.500 --> 15:26.580
This is equivalent to the right now space of A.

15:26.580 --> 15:32.420
As we have also seen in the last lecture that this is essentially a least square operation

15:32.420 --> 15:38.100
when we solve for the Ax equals to 0, solve for the null space of B, what this means is

15:38.100 --> 15:43.860
since the correspondences is corrupted with noise, A would never be exact, it's actually

15:43.860 --> 15:44.980
very noisy.

15:44.980 --> 15:51.980
So if we can get more views like this where we simply fix the camera and we move the calibration

15:51.980 --> 15:56.940
board around, if we can get more views, it will provide a better constraint to this AB

15:56.940 --> 16:03.300
equals to 0 and we will be able to solve for a more accurate B vector over here.

16:03.300 --> 16:09.540
So once B is recovered, we can actually put it back into the capital B, which is actually

16:09.540 --> 16:12.900
a 3x3 matrix.

16:12.900 --> 16:19.860
So small b is actually a 6x1 vector which can be placed back into this symmetric matrix

16:19.860 --> 16:20.860
B.

16:20.860 --> 16:29.900
Finally, k can be obtained because B here is equals to k inverse transpose inverse,

16:29.900 --> 16:34.740
which is equivalent to k transpose k inverse.

16:34.740 --> 16:40.620
We can actually take the inverse of B and then take a Cholesky decomposition on B inverse

16:40.620 --> 16:42.780
to find out the values of k.

16:42.780 --> 16:53.340
So if you take a Cholesky decomposition on a matrix A, this is equivalent to L transpose

16:53.340 --> 16:55.940
or the other way around, it doesn't matter.

16:55.940 --> 17:00.940
So this is lower triangular or the other way around, it could be upper triangular matrix,

17:00.940 --> 17:04.660
which is equivalent to the intrinsic of the camera.

17:04.660 --> 17:12.660
Once k is known, then all we are left with would be just extrinsic parameter of all views.

17:12.660 --> 17:15.700
So now it's no longer just one RNT.

17:15.700 --> 17:22.260
So if we were to take three views, that means that A is at least 6x6, we will have three

17:22.260 --> 17:24.900
views over here, each different view over here.

17:24.900 --> 17:30.220
There will be a different RNT if we have a camera that stays fixed over here that is

17:30.220 --> 17:32.900
looking at these views.

17:32.900 --> 17:38.980
So view 1, you will have an RNT with respect to the camera, view 2 will have another RNT

17:38.980 --> 17:41.140
with respect to the camera, and so on and so forth.

17:41.140 --> 17:48.420
We will also need to solve for all the extrinsic parameters once this is done, as well as the

17:48.420 --> 17:50.480
scale factor.

17:50.480 --> 17:54.820
So now let's look at how to solve for all the remaining unknowns.

17:54.820 --> 18:00.980
So we've seen that earlier on R1 is equivalent to S multiplied by k inverse multiplied by

18:00.980 --> 18:01.980
H1.

18:01.980 --> 18:04.240
So H1 is in that view.

18:04.240 --> 18:15.360
So since k remains fixed for all views, and we know that this from the Kolesky decomposition

18:15.360 --> 18:23.360
earlier on, so we can make use of these values, plug it inside here, and H here would be the

18:23.360 --> 18:31.120
homography, the first column of the homography that is in that particular view that corresponds

18:31.120 --> 18:32.360
to R1 and R2.

18:32.360 --> 18:39.000
For example, if I were to solve R1 here, then H would be taken from this transformation

18:39.000 --> 18:43.760
of the checkerboard onto the image itself.

18:43.760 --> 18:49.400
Then I can solve for this, and R2 I can also solve for this, since this is known and this

18:49.400 --> 18:51.720
is known, this is known and this is known.

18:51.720 --> 18:59.400
R is R1, R2 and R3, and they are all octagonal matrices.

18:59.400 --> 19:01.120
This is octagonal matrix.

19:01.120 --> 19:04.960
This means that the cross product of R1 and R2 will give you R3.

19:04.960 --> 19:13.400
But the only unknown here, the only remaining unknown here is S, which can be easily solved

19:13.400 --> 19:23.140
by taking the scale of this guy over here, because we know that the norm of R1 has to

19:23.140 --> 19:24.560
be equal to 1.

19:24.560 --> 19:30.280
So we can easily solve for this by taking the norm of this guy here, it has to be equal

19:30.280 --> 19:31.920
to 1.

19:31.920 --> 19:37.440
And this is what we get, we can solve for S and put it back into this equation.

19:37.440 --> 19:42.520
So everything here is known, we can easily solve for R1, R2 and R3 for each respective

19:42.520 --> 19:43.520
view.

19:43.520 --> 19:50.480
And finally, the translation vector, which is also an unknown, can also be solved using

19:50.480 --> 19:51.880
this form here.

19:51.880 --> 19:54.400
So now this is known, this is known and this is also known.

19:54.400 --> 19:58.640
We can easily solve for the translation vector.

19:58.640 --> 20:06.120
So what we have looked at so far would be how to solve for all the 11 degrees of freedom

20:06.120 --> 20:09.560
that is in the camera projection matrix.

20:09.560 --> 20:16.840
So there's still another set of unknowns in the camera calibration that we need to solve

20:16.840 --> 20:20.800
for, and that would be what we call the lens distortion.

20:20.800 --> 20:27.400
So usually when we take a picture using any camera, using your cell phone camera or using

20:27.400 --> 20:35.120
the DSLR or even a pocket camera, we will be able to observe some form of distortion

20:35.120 --> 20:36.700
in the image.

20:36.700 --> 20:42.240
So a more commonly observed distortion would be a radial distortion.

20:42.240 --> 20:49.400
For example, if you use a highly convex lens, a fisheye lens, for example, then even for

20:49.400 --> 20:54.280
the normal camera lens, you will be able to observe some form of distortion.

20:54.280 --> 20:59.240
So that it could be a negative radial distortion that looks something like this, all the pixels

20:59.240 --> 21:02.320
will be squashed towards the center.

21:02.320 --> 21:08.960
And or it could also be more commonly observed, it's a positive radial distortion that looks

21:08.960 --> 21:13.160
something like this, where it's distorted with respect to the radial direction, where

21:13.160 --> 21:18.780
the original image should look something like this, where every pixel is regular.

21:18.780 --> 21:24.860
There's also another less commonly observed distortion, which we call the tangential distortion.

21:24.860 --> 21:31.660
So this is more commonly occurring in cheaper cameras than it might be due to manufacturing

21:31.660 --> 21:38.140
imprecision, where let's say this is my photo sensor, and this is my lens that I'm going

21:38.140 --> 21:40.320
to place inside my camera.

21:40.320 --> 21:46.740
So usually due to poor worksmanship, we can see that the camera sensor might not be aligned

21:46.740 --> 21:51.320
in parallel with this lens, it could also be the lens that is misaligned.

21:51.320 --> 21:56.940
So in this case, we will observe tangential distortion because the light rays that is

21:56.940 --> 22:02.860
supposed to be focused onto this, it will be misaligned.

22:02.860 --> 22:09.540
To complete the camera calibration, we also have to model the lens distortion mathematically

22:09.540 --> 22:15.940
and find the parameters that gives us this particular model.

22:15.940 --> 22:20.900
And here, we will first look at how to model the radial distortion.

22:20.900 --> 22:27.220
So let X equals to XY be the image projection of a 3D point without distortion.

22:27.220 --> 22:32.940
This means that I'm looking at this guy here without any distortion, so any point here,

22:32.940 --> 22:38.340
let me represent it with XY, I suppose, which is equals to X over here, this is without

22:38.340 --> 22:39.640
any distortion.

22:39.640 --> 22:45.620
And what we need to do here is that we need to find a formulation, let's say, for example,

22:46.340 --> 22:52.740
the camera has this positive radial distortion, so this is a function that takes in X here,

22:52.740 --> 22:58.300
such that it maps out to Y, where this is the new position, which I call Y here, and

22:58.300 --> 23:03.180
this is the radially distorted pixel location.

23:03.180 --> 23:06.700
And now the question is that what is this particular function?

23:06.700 --> 23:13.500
And we choose to use a parametric way of describing this function over here.

23:13.500 --> 23:20.380
So essentially, this function here, which I called FX earlier on, which takes in the

23:20.380 --> 23:24.920
pixel without distortion and maps it to a pixel with distortion.

23:24.920 --> 23:30.900
So FX here is essentially given by this equation that is first proposed by Brown in this particular

23:30.900 --> 23:34.260
paper, closed range camera calibration.

23:34.260 --> 23:39.980
And we can see that the input to this would be XY, the pixel location without distortion.

23:39.980 --> 23:50.880
And there's a parameterization here, where 1 plus K1R2 plus K2R4 plus K5R6, where R here

23:50.880 --> 23:56.900
is simply the circle equation, R2 equals to X2 plus Y2.

23:56.900 --> 24:04.820
So R is actually a function of X and Y, which is this guy over here.

24:04.820 --> 24:11.560
And then here, K1, K2, K3 is basically the radial distortion where we use to parameterize

24:11.560 --> 24:13.520
this mapping function.

24:13.520 --> 24:19.980
These are unknowns that need to be found during the calibration process.

24:19.980 --> 24:26.080
We can also model the tangential distortion using this particular model here that was

24:26.080 --> 24:31.620
also proposed in the same paper by Brown in 1971.

24:31.620 --> 24:39.420
But in comparison to the radial distortion, where we find a mapping function that maps

24:39.420 --> 24:49.300
a pixel without distortion onto a pixel with distortion, here we say that we are going

24:49.300 --> 24:55.720
to find the delta change in the pixel after the distortion.

24:55.720 --> 25:01.880
That means that if I were to give you an image, and then this point here, which I call

25:01.880 --> 25:05.600
X and Y, it has no distortion.

25:05.600 --> 25:13.440
So in the previous case, after some form of distortion over here, I'm going to map this

25:13.440 --> 25:18.540
and the mapping function that I looked at earlier on for the radial distortion is to

25:18.540 --> 25:25.720
find the exact location of this with XR with radial distortion, YR with radial distortion.

25:25.720 --> 25:33.100
But now this case here, I'm going to find the delta change, which adds on to the radial

25:33.100 --> 25:35.860
distortion, which I call DX over here.

25:35.860 --> 25:43.320
So this essentially is also a function of X, which is my perfect pixel on the image

25:43.320 --> 25:45.060
without any distortion.

25:45.060 --> 25:53.020
But after mapping to here, what I'm finding DX over here is essentially the new location

25:53.020 --> 25:59.220
of the pixel after I take the radial distortion into account.

25:59.220 --> 26:06.460
So the final location here would be simply XR plus DX over here.

26:06.460 --> 26:12.820
And DX is given by this equation, where R is still the same, R squared equals to X squared

26:12.820 --> 26:14.200
plus Y squared.

26:14.200 --> 26:19.040
And now we have two more unknowns, which we call K3 and K4.

26:19.040 --> 26:26.560
All together, I would have five unknown parameters, K1, K2, K5 for the radial distortion and K3,

26:26.560 --> 26:29.520
K4 for the tangential distortion.

26:29.520 --> 26:41.880
So this would be the final equation where I can map a perfect pixel onto the tangentially

26:41.880 --> 26:50.380
and radially distorted image.

26:50.380 --> 26:55.360
So it will be given by this guy, which is this equation.

26:55.360 --> 27:01.400
Now the question is that how can we find K1, K2, K3, K4 and K5?

27:01.400 --> 27:05.200
So we'll use a very simple method to do this.

27:05.200 --> 27:10.960
We'll just put it through an iterative refinement, a maximum likelihood estimation.

27:10.960 --> 27:20.100
So the steps to estimate this would be left to the last after we have estimated the Intrinsics

27:20.100 --> 27:26.480
and Extrinsics calibration using the method proposed by Zhang Zhenyu that we have seen

27:26.480 --> 27:27.560
earlier on.

27:27.560 --> 27:34.160
And then once we get these unknowns, the K as well as the Ri and Ti, because of different

27:34.160 --> 27:40.160
views of the calibration toolbox, we can simply put it into this equation here, this cost

27:40.160 --> 27:45.500
function here, which we call the reprojection error.

27:45.500 --> 27:50.160
So what this means is that this guy here, pi, is simply the projection function.

27:50.160 --> 27:55.840
So for example, I have an image here, I have a point here where I know that this point

27:55.840 --> 28:02.240
corresponds to the checkerboard, the corner of a checkerboard, and it's being reprojected

28:02.240 --> 28:03.240
here.

28:03.240 --> 28:11.240
So let's call this point xj, and let's call this reprojected point xij, where j corresponds

28:11.240 --> 28:16.740
to the 3D point and i corresponds to the camera view over here.

28:16.740 --> 28:22.460
So this means that I'm reprojecting this point, which is the jth point on the checkerboard,

28:22.460 --> 28:28.700
onto the i camera view, and the correspondence will be given by xij over here.

28:28.700 --> 28:35.700
So this reprojection has to undergo p of x, which we have seen earlier on.

28:35.700 --> 28:43.300
So p here would consist of these three sets of parameters k, r, and t.

28:43.300 --> 28:50.540
But then once we use this p to reproject xj back onto the image, this is on the assumption

28:50.540 --> 28:54.460
that the mapping is perfect.

28:54.460 --> 28:56.260
That means that there's no distortion.

28:56.260 --> 29:02.960
So what we need to do to compensate for the distortion would be simply putting this p

29:02.960 --> 29:10.180
xj into the distortion function that we have seen earlier on here, to get the new distorted

29:10.180 --> 29:11.180
location.

29:11.180 --> 29:18.160
And since there's a set of unknowns, which consists of the five parameters in k, here

29:18.160 --> 29:23.320
we have an equation that is a function of the five unknowns over here, and p here we

29:23.320 --> 29:27.200
assume it to be known already, because we would have already obtained it from the first

29:27.200 --> 29:29.180
step of the calibration.

29:29.180 --> 29:36.300
So now the question is that, once we reproject this, so here we can think of this as a, it's

29:36.300 --> 29:46.760
actually a function of p xj as well as the unknown of k, which is this guy over here.

29:46.760 --> 29:53.160
And then, so once we reproject it based on this equation that consists of all the unknowns

29:53.160 --> 30:02.320
of k1 to k5, so we can compare this reprojected point onto the image, the xij that we have

30:02.320 --> 30:07.280
observed on the image, essentially we want to minimize the square errors here.

30:07.280 --> 30:12.160
And we'll see in the lecture that when we talk more about bundle adjustment, how to

30:12.160 --> 30:15.600
make use of lower Markov to minimize this.

30:15.600 --> 30:23.040
This is essentially a continuous unconstrained optimization, which can be done using lower

30:23.040 --> 30:24.040
Markov.

30:24.040 --> 30:29.780
We'll do also a refinement over r and t as well as k, and then we'll find this where

30:29.780 --> 30:31.580
we initialize it to zero.

30:31.580 --> 30:36.340
The reason why we can initialize this to zero is because these parameters are usually very

30:36.340 --> 30:38.240
small, it's close to zero.

30:38.240 --> 30:44.920
So zero initialization is actually a pretty good initialization in this sense.

30:44.920 --> 30:51.240
Then once we put it through, we'll be able to get a new estimate of all the parameters,

30:51.240 --> 30:56.640
all the intrinsic parameters, extrinsic parameters, as well as all the unknown parameters that

30:56.640 --> 31:01.140
parameterizes the radial and tangential distortion here.

31:01.140 --> 31:10.060
So this is also called minimizing the reprojection error because we are reprojecting the 3D points

31:10.060 --> 31:15.460
onto the 2D image, so what this point here is being reprojected here, the correspondence

31:15.460 --> 31:19.580
might be here, there's an error here that we want to minimize.

31:19.580 --> 31:27.220
So this is, in other words, also a geometric error that we have seen earlier on in the

31:27.220 --> 31:34.060
last lecture, that this kind of error, minimizing the geometric error, is far more accurate

31:34.060 --> 31:37.260
than what we have seen earlier on using Tanzanio's method.

31:37.260 --> 31:45.480
We were solving a b equals to zero over there, and this is actually the algebraic error.

31:45.480 --> 31:49.620
We have seen earlier on in the lecture on homography that this error is less accurate

31:49.620 --> 31:56.200
than this error, hence by applying this, minimizing the reprojection error at the last step, we

31:56.200 --> 32:01.920
are guaranteed to have much more accurate results for the calibration.

32:01.920 --> 32:08.700
So once we have gotten the calibration for the radial and tangential distortion, we can

32:08.700 --> 32:11.200
actually do the mapping the other way around.

32:11.200 --> 32:16.680
So previously I mentioned that there's a function here, it's essentially this direction, where

32:16.680 --> 32:22.640
any point here x without any distortion, we are going to put it through f and then we

32:22.640 --> 32:27.380
will get this point here, which corresponds to the y here which is the distorted.

32:27.380 --> 32:33.600
So this is with distortion, and this is without any distortion.

32:33.600 --> 32:39.120
So once this is done, once we know exactly this function, we can actually compute a lookout

32:39.120 --> 32:40.740
table as mentioned before.

32:40.740 --> 32:48.860
So every pixel here, compute a lookout table that look up on the corresponding pixel over

32:48.860 --> 32:53.460
here, and then I can simply fill this part in.

32:53.460 --> 32:58.460
So originally we will not be able to know this, this is what we observe, but we will

32:58.460 --> 33:04.800
compute for every pixel here, every pixel value here, we will compute the corresponding

33:04.800 --> 33:10.340
y and then use this as a lookout table to fill in the entries here.

33:10.340 --> 33:20.540
So eventually we'll be able to recover the undistorted image, which we call lens distortion

33:20.540 --> 33:22.780
correction.

33:22.780 --> 33:29.980
So this ends my lecture, and in summary, today we have looked at how to describe the camera

33:29.980 --> 33:36.540
projection with pinhole camera model, that's the most basic camera model that we can use

33:36.540 --> 33:39.320
to describe projection matrix.

33:39.320 --> 33:44.800
And then we also have looked at how to identify the camera center, principal planes, principal

33:44.800 --> 33:50.040
points, and principal axes from the camera projection matrix.

33:50.040 --> 33:57.520
We also looked at how to use the camera projection matrix to find the forward and backward projection.

33:57.520 --> 34:04.840
Then next we looked at the properties of and some definitions of the commonly seen affine

34:04.840 --> 34:05.840
camera.

34:05.840 --> 34:12.040
And finally, we looked at a technique proposed by Zhang Zhenyou to do calibration to find

34:12.040 --> 34:15.880
the intrinsic and extrinsic values of a projective camera.

34:15.880 --> 34:21.000
Furthermore, we also looked at the radial and tangential distortion, and also how to

34:21.000 --> 34:27.880
get this radial and tangential distortion parameters from calibration, and how to undo

34:27.880 --> 34:30.160
the distortion effect.

34:30.160 --> 34:30.520
Thank you.

